Z||000000||RossT||10-22-1999||02:53 AM||||<BR>I am thinking of placing an order for these<BR>two books from the Fiero Store. Does any<BR>one out there own these?  If so, what do<BR>you think?<P>Thanks  Ross||||reg||1
Z||000001||ChadMan||10-22-1999||10:13 AM||||I own both books. Fiero by Witzenberg is an excellent, five-star, must-own book for any Fiero nut.  It is full of great info and full-color pix.  The focus is largely on the development and engineering of the Fiero, plus there's good info on Fiero racing history and model-year changes.<P>The press guide is interesting only if you eat, sleep, and breathe Fieros.  It is a collection of damn near everything Pontiac's PR people released concerning the Fiero.  As a result, much more than half the book is devoted to the 1984 model.  Much of it is just hyped-up press releases or dry technical material, though it's interesting from a historical standpoint to watch as the Fiero went from center stage to being the automotive equivalent of Rodney Dangerfield.<P>My advice:  Order the Witzenberg book (and if you don't already have it, the Brooklands Fiero book).  If you're really hardcore when it comes to Fieros, order the press guide too.||||reg||14
Z||000002||RossT||10-26-1999||03:12 AM||||I just recieved my brooklands Fiero 1984-1988.  I found the acknowledgements very interesting:<P>"We first published this book in 1988 and<BR>reprinted it in 1991. Here we are in 1993<BR>having to print it again. It is intersting that<BR>the two books that we published at the same time covering the Toyota MR2 and the Honda CRX have still not sold out of the original printing although they were deemed to be better cars at that time. <P>A clue as to its future place in automotive spectrum is given by Autoweek in its obituary. "Sales of Fiero my be bolstered by collectors. An 88 with the V6 and 5 speed may be the most highly valued Fiero for collectors in 10 years hence."<P>It is our view that the Fiero will quickly go<BR>to  classic status as did and earlier British 2-seater the Triump Stag....... <P>Ten years from now the Fiero will be greatly<BR>sought after, firstly because of it rarity, shape and 2 seather configuration, secondly, because its plastic body will be<BR>free from rust and finally because most of its mechanicals will be readily abailable.<P>We predict a long second life for the Fiero and have no doubt that we will have to keep this book in print for many years to<BR>come."<P>I will order the Witzenburg book from the Fiero Store, but will hold of on the Press<BR>guide for now.<P>Thanks CHADMAN,  Ross.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick||10-26-1999||04:00 AM||||<BR>I agree with ChadMan, <B>Fiero by Witzenburg</B> is a great book. I had borrowed it from the library a couple of years ago when I first bought my Fiero, and I found it to be quite fascinating. It's one of the few books I've ever borrowed that I've often thought that I should treat myself and buy a copy. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
